    title: "MCP Client Analytics: Understand Who Uses Your Server",
    tag: "MCP analytics",
    excerpt: "Use MCP client analytics to separate adoption, compatibility, reliability, and support work by Claude, Cursor, ChatGPT, custom clients, and environments.",
    keywords: ["MCP client analytics", "MCP client mix", "which clients use my MCP server", "MCP usage by client"],
    answer: "MCP client analytics tells you which hosts connect, what they discover, which tools they call, and where their behavior differs. Use client identity as a dimension for compatibility and support, not as a proxy for model quality. Client names may be missing or user-provided, so make identity confidence visible.",
    takeaway: "The same MCP server can have different reliability and adoption profiles by client; global averages hide that split.",
    sections: [
      { heading: "Define client identity carefully", body: "Record the client name and version when the protocol or server can observe it, but distinguish declared metadata from verified identity. A custom client may omit or spoof a name. Keep the source, timestamp, and confidence of the identity, and do not collect unnecessary user or prompt data to fill the gap.", bullets: ["Client name and version when provided.", "Transport, protocol revision, and auth mode.", "Environment and server release.", "Identity source and confidence."] },
      { heading: "Compare behavior, not popularity", body: "A client mix chart is only the beginning. Compare discovery, tool selection, result errors, latency, retries, cancellations, and workflow outcomes by client. A small client cohort can expose a schema or transport compatibility regression before it appears in a global metric.", bullets: ["Connection and negotiation success by client.", "Catalog and tool adoption differences.", "Error and latency distributions.", "Outcome completion and retry amplification."] },
      { heading: "Use client differences to prioritize fixes", body: "If one client fails after a protocol or schema change, reproduce through that client and the production gateway. Keep the server contract stable where possible, document supported revisions, and make a client-specific workaround explicit. Do not silently degrade to a legacy behavior that creates ambiguous evidence.", bullets: ["Keep a tested client compatibility matrix.", "Compare before and after protocol or catalog changes.", "Communicate unsupported revisions clearly.", "Route support issues to evidence, not anecdotes."] },
    ],
    faq: [["Can server analytics see the model used by a client?", "Not necessarily. Server-boundary analytics can observe client metadata and requests; private host or provider model details require a separate integration."], ["What if client names are missing?", "Keep them unknown rather than guessing. Use transport, auth, release, and other bounded dimensions to investigate the cohort."], ["How should I handle spoofed names?", "Treat client identity as a useful but untrusted dimension. Do not grant authorization or infer model behavior from it alone."]],
    related: ["understanding-your-mcp-client-mix", "mcp-tool-usage-report", "mcp-observability-vs-llm-observability"],
